  "summary": "The dollar is firm and stretched, core yields are high, and equities are still holding up while oil sits well below the triple-digit levels in older books. On the latest fixings WTI is 88.56 and Brent is 95.84, gold is 3886, and the S&P is 7562 with the VIX at 18.7. Euro-dollar at 1.1052 and the kiwi at 0.5532 are at multi-year lows, and dollar-yen is at 160. The US two-year is 4.88 and the ten-year is 5.15. Official SOFR is 3.90, while futures price a higher path, near 4.31 by December and about 4.60 by March 2027. Monday commentary talks about softer Treasuries ahead of Chair Warsh, but the last verified yield fixing is still Friday's backup. Ukraine's strike on the Crimean Bridge keeps a geopolitical bid in the energy story without lifting Brent back to those older levels. Canada inflation arrives after this cutoff, then Australian wages and euro-area prices midweek, with the Fed, the Bank of Canada, and the Reserve Bank of New Zealand on 9 December.",
